{"builder":"## Criteria the build must meet (expect lines, word for word)\n1. Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\n2. In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\n3. Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\n4. Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\"port\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\n5. `npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\n\n## Limits (non-goals)\n- Making `loadConfig` swallow parse errors or return `{}` or the defaults for a malformed file.\n- Changing how a missing config file is reported (ENOENT), beyond keeping it an exit-1 `bad config:` failure.\n- Validating config values or schema (e.g. port type).\n- Supporting comments, JSON5 or other lenient config formats.\n\n## Steps\n1. Read `src/config.js`, `src/app.js`, `test/config.test.js` and `README.md`. `loadConfig(path)` currently returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`; `src/app.js` prints `bad config: <error.message>` to stderr and exits 1. Do not adopt the issue's suggested fix of catching and returning `{}`.\n2. In `src/config.js`, read the file, then wrap only the `JSON.parse` call in try/catch. On a parse error, rethrow an error (preferably still a `SyntaxError`, so the existing `bad JSON throws` test keeps passing; set `cause` to the original error) whose `message` contains the path exactly as passed (do not resolve it to an absolute path), plus the line and column of the mistake, e.g. `<path>:3:3: <original message>` or `<path> line 3 column 3: ...`.\n3. Compute line and column yourself so the output does not depend only on Node's message: take the character position from the original error (match `position (\\d+)` in its message), and count newlines in the text before that position to get a 1-based line and a 1-based column. If Node's message already has `(line N column M)`, you may use that, but keep the self-computed fallback for Node versions that give only a position. For the file `{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n` the result must be line 3, column 3. If no position can be found, still include the path and the original message.\n4. Leave read errors (e.g. ENOENT) as they are; they must still lead to an exit-1 `bad config:` line. Leave `src/app.js` printing `bad config: ${error.message}` and exiting 1, changing it only if needed. Valid JSON must load exactly as before.\n5. Add tests in `test/config.test.js` (node:test): one that writes the four-line c.json content above to a temp file and asserts that `loadConfig` throws, that the error message includes the exact temp path, and that it shows line 3 and column 3; one for `{port: 9000` asserting the message includes the path. These must fail against the current `src/config.js`. Keep the existing tests passing. Optionally add a test that spawns `node src/app.js` on the bad file and checks exit 1, empty stdout and the stderr contents.\n6. Optionally update `README.md` to say the `bad config:` line names the file and location.\n7. Run `npm test` on Node 22, and run `node src/app.js` by hand on the bad and ok files to confirm exit codes, stdout and stderr match criteria 1, 2 and 4.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head from the repository root, using Node v22 (`node --version`). Create files with printf in a fresh `mktemp -d` directory, written below as `$d`.\n\n### Check 1: malformed config exits 1 with empty stdout\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; wc -c < $d/out; cat $d/out`\nPASS: exit code is 1 and `$d/out` is empty (0 bytes, so no `listening on` line).\nFAIL: any other exit code, or anything on stdout.\n\n### Check 2: stderr names the path, line 3 and column 3\nUsing the same run as Check 1, run `cat $d/err` and check it with grep -F for the `$d/c.json` string.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the string `$d/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line (not changed in form), and shows line number 3 and column number 3 of the mistake in a readable form (e.g. `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, `line 3, column 3`).\nFAIL: any of these is missing, or the line/column shown is not 3 and 3.\n\n### Check 3: loadConfig throws with the path in the message\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: prints `OK`.\nFAIL: prints `RETURNED ...` (exit 2) or `NO PATH: ...`, or crashes some other way.\n\n### Check 4: valid config still works\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(loadConfig('$d/ok.json')))\"`\nPASS: the app prints `listening on 9000` on stdout and exits 0, and the second command prints `{\"port\":9000}` (deep-equal to `{ port: 9000 }`, no extra keys).\nFAIL: any other output, exit code or returned value.\n\n### Check 5: npm test passes and a new test catches the bug\nRun `npm test` at the PR head. Then find the base commit (`git merge-base HEAD <default branch>`), run `git checkout <base> -- src` so the base `src/` sits with the PR's `test/`, run `npm test` again, then restore with `git checkout HEAD -- src`.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the PR head; with base `src/`, at least one test fails, and that failing test checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path (confirm by reading the test).\nFAIL: `npm test` fails at head, or no test fails against base `src/`, or none of the failing tests checks the file path in the error.\n\n## Advisory\n- Read `src/config.js` and `src/app.js` to see whether line/column come only from Node's message text (for example, the code just passes through `error.message`). If they do, note it as a portability risk for Node versions that give only a position. This does not affect the PASS/FAIL verdicts above.\n- Note it if `loadConfig` swallows errors or returns defaults in any branch, if a missing file (ENOENT) no longer exits 1 with `bad config:`, or if the thrown error is no longer a `SyntaxError`."}